<mujoco model="swing_robot">
  <!-- Second physical redesign (see README section documenting this), built
       from the user's real-world hardware dimensions rather than the earlier
       visual-diagram estimates. Structurally different from the previous
       model: the rope now attaches RIGIDLY to a combined seat/upper-leg
       unit at an OFFSET point along its length (not at the hip hinge
       itself), and the hip/knee hinges live at opposite ends of that unit.
       This was a "fresh model, not a continuation" promotion --
       archive_old_model.py already moved the prior training lineage aside
       (111.6M steps archived), so train.py takes the fresh-start branch.

       Body tree, top to bottom:
         pivot -> swing_arm (rope, swing_hinge) -> seat (RIGID, no joint)
           -> torso (lean_hinge) -> head (RIGID, no joint -- see head's
              comment below for why "fixed hinge" isn't literally a
              zero-range joint here)
           -> lower_leg (knee_hinge) -> leg_mass (position tunable)

       Key design point (per the user, "probably the most critical
       modification"): the rope attaches to the seat/upper-leg unit at a
       point BETWEEN the hip and knee hinges, not at either end -- 0.06m
       from the hip (lean_hinge), 0.14m from the knee (knee_hinge), summing
       to the unit's own 0.20m length.

       Rider's arms are still deliberately NOT modeled -- per the user,
       they're not meant to do any work (grip only), so their length is
       physically irrelevant to the dynamics and there's nothing to gain
       from representing them.

       CORRECTED (see the seat body's own comment below for the full
       explanation): the seat/upper-leg unit runs PERPENDICULAR to the
       rope (local X), not inline with it (local Z) -- an error in the
       original text description of this geometry that went unnoticed
       until after a training round on the inline version. Only the
       seat/torso/lower_leg attachment axis changed; the rope itself, the
       rope attachment point, and every joint's range/axis/damping are
       unaffected.
  -->

  <option gravity="0 0 -9.81" timestep="0.005"/>

  <default>
    <joint damping="0.02"/>
    <geom rgba="0.7 0.7 0.7 1"/>
  </default>

  <worldbody>
    <light diffuse="0.8 0.8 0.8" pos="0 0 5" dir="0 0 -1"/>
    <geom name="ground" type="plane" size="5 5 0.1" rgba="0.85 0.9 0.85 1"/>

    <!-- Fixed pivot: the top bar of the swing set -->
    <body name="pivot" pos="0 0 3">
      <geom type="sphere" size="0.03" rgba="0.2 0.2 0.2 1"/>

      <!-- Swing arm / rope, free to rotate about the Y axis. Called "rope"
           in conversation/comments per the user's preference, though the
           body/joint names stay swing_arm/swing_hinge for continuity with
           swing_env.py, which reads these names directly. -->
      <body name="swing_arm" pos="0 0 0">
        <joint name="swing_hinge" type="hinge" axis="0 1 0" pos="0 0 0"
               range="-165 165" damping="0.01"/>
        <!-- ROPE_LENGTH = 2.25m -- midpoint of the user's stated 2.0-2.5m
             range, not yet pinned to a final value. Easy one-number change
             here (and the matching body pos two lines down, which MUST
             move together -- the seat attaches at the rope's far end).
             swing_env.py's self.arm_length (used in the reward's height
             calculation) is kept in sync with this value -- if this
             changes, that must change too. mass=0.5 explicit override --
             default density on this thin a capsule was producing 1.6kg,
             unrealistically heavy for an actual rope/chain and not
             dynamically negligible at the end of a 2.25m arm. 0.5kg per
             the user. -->
        <geom type="capsule" fromto="0 0 0  0 0 -2.25" size="0.015"
              rgba="0.3 0.3 0.3 1" mass="0.5"/>

        <!-- Seat / upper-leg unit: ONE rigid body per the user ("think of
             them as glued together"), attached RIGIDLY to the rope's end
             -- no joint here at all, matching "the rope attaches to a seat
             with no joint." This body's local origin IS the rope
             attachment point. The hip (lean_hinge, below) and knee
             (knee_hinge, below) live at opposite ends of this unit, NOT at
             its origin -- the 0.06/0.14 split straddles the rope
             attachment point rather than sitting to one side of it. -->
        <body name="seat" pos="0 0 -2.25">
          <!-- CORRECTED, in-line-with-rope -> perpendicular-to-rope: the
               user caught this after the fact -- the seat/upper-leg unit
               was described in text as attaching to the rope at an offset
               point along ITS OWN length, but the geometry below had it
               running along the SAME axis as the rope (local Z, straight
               up/down), which is not what a real seat looks like. A seat
               sits crosswise under the rope, not as a straight-line
               continuation of it. Fixed by rotating this whole unit 90 deg
               within the swing plane -- local Z -> local X. The rope still
               hangs along Z (unaffected), and this body's own pos="0 0
               -2.25" (the rope attachment point, set on the PARENT body
               tag above) is unchanged; only the geom/children below it
               are rotated. Visual/mass geom for the unit itself: spans
               from the hip end (+0.06) to the knee end (-0.14), passing
               through the rope attachment at its own local origin (0,0,0)
               -- same offsets as before, just along a different axis.
               mass=1.5 is a reasoned starting value, not derived from
               anything -- represents the seat structure, rope-attachment
               hardware, and (eventually) the hip servo's housing. Open to
               change. -->
          <geom type="capsule" fromto="0.06 0 0  -0.14 0 0" size="0.025"
                rgba="0.5 0.4 0.3 1" mass="1.5"/>

          <!-- Hip joint. pos="0 0 0" here is in the CHILD (torso) body's
               own frame -- torso's body pos below (0.06 0 0) is what
               actually places this hinge 0.06m from the rope attachment,
               to one side (local +X, perpendicular to the rope -- see the
               CORRECTED comment above; this used to say "toward the
               pivot", which was only true under the old, incorrect
               inline-with-rope orientation). Range/damping carried over
               unchanged from the previous model -- the user's dimensions
               didn't specify new values for these, and they deserve their
               own re-examination now that the geometry itself is
               confirmed (a past investigation found the old model's
               -5/+80 range severely constrained one side of the pump;
               that finding was measured against a differently-structured
               geometry, so it may not transfer cleanly here -- worth
               re-testing, not assumed to still hold). -->
          <body name="torso" pos="0.06 0 0">
            <joint name="lean_hinge" type="hinge" axis="0 1 0" pos="0 0 0"
                   range="-5 80" damping="0.05"/>
            <!-- Torso length 0.38m (lean_hinge to head), per the user.
                 mass=3.0 is the user's own suggested starting value.
                 Capsule instead of the old box -- radius 0.05 is a
                 reasoned placeholder for torso thickness, not measured. -->
            <geom type="capsule" fromto="0 0 0  0 0 0.38" size="0.05"
                  rgba="0.8 0.2 0.2 1" mass="3.0"/>

            <!-- Head: separate body per the user ("keep them separate but
                 with a fixed hinge for now"), attached at torso's far end.
                 MuJoCo won't accept a zero-width limited range (tried
                 range="0 0", errors: "range[0] should be smaller than
                 range[1]") so a real-but-locked joint isn't expressible as
                 cleanly as hoped -- simplest correct option is no <joint>
                 element at all for now, i.e. truly rigid, same as how the
                 rest of this file treats any unarticulated connection.
                 Adding real neck articulation later is still a small,
                 well-scoped change (insert a <joint name="neck_hinge"
                 type="hinge" .../> here with a real range) -- just not a
                 zero-effort one-line toggle the way range="0 0" would have
                 been. size 0.15m read as DIAMETER (radius 0.075) --
                 human-head-scale, per the user's "doesn't matter except
                 for representation." mass=0.4 is a small reasoned
                 placeholder, not something the user specified -- the
                 least-certain number in this file, easiest to ignore/zero
                 out if it's not worth modeling separately from torso mass
                 at all. -->
            <body name="head" pos="0 0 0.38">
              <geom name="head_geom" type="sphere" size="0.075"
                    rgba="0.9 0.7 0.6 1" mass="0.4"/>
            </body>
          </body>

          <!-- Knee joint, 0.14m from the rope attachment, on the opposite
               side from the hip (local -X, same CORRECTED perpendicular
               axis as the hip/seat geom above -- this used to say "away
               from the pivot", which was only true under the old,
               incorrect inline-with-rope orientation). Range/damping
               carried over unchanged from the previous model for the same
               reason as lean_hinge above -- not re-derived, flagged for
               re-examination now that geometry is locked in. -->
          <body name="lower_leg" pos="-0.14 0 0">
            <!-- UPDATED, -60/+60 -> -60/+90, after the user asked to allow
                 full extension. Zero here is NOT inline with the thigh --
                 it's the shin hanging straight down, perpendicular to the
                 horizontal thigh (verified directly against the compiled
                 model: at knee=0 the shin tip sits at local (0,-0.30)
                 relative to the knee, i.e. straight down). Negative angles
                 swing the shin back toward the hip, tucking it under the
                 seat (at -60, the shin tip lands almost directly below the
                 hip); positive angles swing it away from the hip, toward
                 full extension. +90 was chosen as "fully extended" per the
                 user; -60 (unchanged) was already established as a
                 reasonable tuck limit. -->
            <joint name="knee_hinge" type="hinge" axis="0 1 0"
                   range="-45 90" damping="0.2"/>
            <!-- 5.9ae: lower limit -60 -> -45. The real knee mount binds at ~45 deg
                 of tuck. Earlier -60 reasoning above is kept as history. -->
            <!-- Lower leg length 0.30m (up from 0.12m in the previous
                 model), per the user. Structural rod only -- default
                 density, no explicit mass override, matching how this
                 file treats connecting rods vs. the deliberate point
                 masses. -->
            <geom type="capsule" fromto="0 0 0  0 0 -0.30" size="0.02"
                  rgba="0.3 0.3 0.3 1"/>
            <!-- Leg mass: the user said "weight can be placed at any point
                 on the leg" -- the position below (currently -0.30, the
                 far end, matching the previous model's convention of an
                 end-loaded mass, which maximizes lever arm per kg) is the
                 one number to move to test that. mass UPDATED, 1.0 -> 1.5kg,
                 per the user (the original 1.0 was judged too low). -->
            <geom name="leg_mass" type="sphere" size="0.05" pos="0 0 -0.30"
                  rgba="0.2 0.2 0.8 1" mass="1.5"/>
          </body>
        </body>
      </body>
    </body>
  </worldbody>

  <actuator>
    <!-- forcerange values CAPPED (README 5.9ac), superseding the 5.9ab
         full-torque update directly below: the real ordered STS3095-C001
         servos are individually rated ~9.3 Nm (lean, single unit) / 18.6
         Nm (knee, synchronized pair) -- see 5.9ab's math -- but running
         training (and the real hardware) at that full rating was tried
         directly and caused two consecutive real failures: first a severe
         overshoot regression (peak angle 117-121 deg vs. the 67.5 deg
         target), then, after several more training rounds reacting to
         that overshoot penalty, a bootstrap-failure collapse (9-14 of 15
         eval episodes failing to bootstrap the swing at all) that did not
         self-correct across 3 further rounds -- a stall, not a transient
         dip. Root cause: forcerange is a torque CEILING on this project's
         PD position actuators, not a speed setting -- the old, lower
         ceiling meant the controller was routinely saturation-limited
         (see the old reasoning below), so the trained policy's action
         outputs implicitly assumed that ceiling. Removing it let the same
         commanded positions produce far more real torque and speed than
         the policy was ever tuned against, which is what broke both the
         overshoot behavior and (once the policy over-corrected against
         that penalty) the delicate resonant-pumping capture basin this
         project has separately diagnosed as fragile (5.9h).

         Fix: cap forcerange back to the exact values the current lineage
         was actually trained and validated against -- knee 5.4 Nm, lean
         8.1 Nm (unchanged from the pre-5.9ab placeholder below) -- rather
         than inventing new untested intermediate numbers. This is not
         just a training-stability stopgap: the user separately confirmed
         a preference for the resulting slower, less abrupt/"robotic"
         motion this produces, so unlike a normal temporary derate, there
         is no default plan to raise this back toward the servos' full
         rating later -- it may simply stay here. REAL-WORLD ACTION
         REQUIRED: forcerange in this file has no effect on the physical
         servos on its own -- the deployed STS3095s need to be
         firmware-limited (via their serial-bus torque/current-limit
         register) to the same ~5.4/8.1 Nm ceiling for real behavior to
         match what's trained here; this leaves generous headroom below
         their ~9.3/18.6 Nm rating, which is also good practice for
         thermal/longevity margin rather than running them at their stall
         ceiling continuously. Mounting/power-supply consistency, the
         actual reason STS3095 was chosen for all three positions, is
         fully retained either way -- that benefit never depended on
         using the servos' full torque rating.

         PREVIOUS reasoning below (5.9ab, kept for history -- the
         full-torque update this entry caps back down from):
         forcerange values UPDATED (README 5.9ab) to reflect REAL ordered
         hardware, superseding the placeholder reasoning further below:
         Feetech STS3095-C001 (7.4V variant), 95 kgf-cm stall torque each
         = ~9.3 Nm (95 * 0.0980665 kgf-cm->Nm conversion). User ordered
         STS3095 for ALL THREE servo positions (1 lean/torso, 2 knee
         synchronized pair) rather than mixed models -- a deliberate
         tradeoff, not an oversight: knee ends up meaningfully overpowered
         (18.6 Nm combined vs. the ~5.4 Nm this project found sufficient
         there) in exchange for uniform mounting hardware and one shared
         power-supply spec across every servo on the robot. lean_motor:
         single unit, 9.3 Nm. knee_motor: synchronized pair, 2 * 9.3 =
         18.6 Nm combined. kp=40 deliberately UNCHANGED throughout all of
         this -- it's a simulation-tuned position-servo gain from testing
         that gave clean tracking, not a value derived from any specific
         real servo's own internal control loop, so a real hardware
         choice doesn't change what it should be.

         PREVIOUS reasoning below (kept for history -- describes the
         placeholder these numbers replace, from before real parts were
         ordered):
         forcerange values below are grounded in real parts, not arbitrary:
         Feetech's higher-torque STS3215 variant (C044) is ~2.69 Nm stall
         torque each (27.4 kgf-cm), a genuine budget option discussed with
         the user. A synchronized, mechanically-coupled PAIR of these
         combines to roughly 5.4 Nm -- validated by direct simulation as
         sufficient for knee, which only needed ~5 Nm to pump well. Lean
         needed more (~8 Nm threshold below which it can't pump at all,
         found via direct sweep) -- two of the same servo falls short
         there, so this uses THREE (~8.1 Nm combined) as the lean
         placeholder instead. Final servo count/model is still an open
         decision on the user's end ("I'll stew on the servos") -- these
         are reasoned interim values for training purposes, not a locked
         hardware spec. kp=40 carried over from testing that gave clean
         tracking; behavior at this lower forcerange is more
         saturation-dominated than at the higher value it was tuned
         against, which is realistic for actual torque-limited hardware,
         not a modeling artifact to fix. -->
    <position name="lean_motor" joint="lean_hinge" kp="40" dampratio="1"
              forcerange="-8.1 8.1" ctrlrange="-0.0873 1.3963"/>
    <!-- ctrlrange UPDATED to match knee_hinge's -60/+90 range above (was
         -60/+60): -60 deg stays -1.0472 rad, +90 deg is 1.5708 rad. Kept in
         radians, matching lean_motor's convention below, since MuJoCo
         actuator ctrlrange isn't auto-converted by the compiler's angle
         setting the way <joint range> is -- has to be hand-computed and
         hand-updated any time the joint range changes, which is exactly
         what this edit is doing. -->
    <position name="knee_motor" joint="knee_hinge" kp="40" dampratio="1"
              forcerange="-5.4 5.4" ctrlrange="-0.7854 1.5708"/>
    <!-- 5.9ae: knee ctrlrange low end -1.0472 (-60 deg) -> -0.7854 (-45 deg),
         matching the joint range change above (real hardware binds at ~45). -->
  </actuator>

  <sensor>
    <jointpos name="swing_angle" joint="swing_hinge"/>
    <jointvel name="swing_vel" joint="swing_hinge"/>
    <jointpos name="lean_angle" joint="lean_hinge"/>
    <jointvel name="lean_vel" joint="lean_hinge"/>
    <jointpos name="knee_angle" joint="knee_hinge"/>
    <jointvel name="knee_vel" joint="knee_hinge"/>
  </sensor>
</mujoco>
